        heading: "Civil Engineering: Grading, Drainage, Utilities, and Access",
        body: "The civil plans turn the approved site concept into buildable infrastructure. Grading establishes the building pads, parking grades, and ADA-accessible routes; the drainage design sizes inlets, storm sewer, and detention or conveyance so the developed site handles its runoff without flooding neighbors or overwhelming downstream systems; and the utility plan extends water and sewer to each lot with the easements the city requires. Paving sections follow the city's standards for the traffic the site will carry, and the erosion-control plan keeps sediment on site during construction under the applicable stormwater permits.\n\nAccess design deserves early attention because it is hard to fix late. Driveway locations on arterials like Kenosha Street or Aspen Avenue must meet spacing and width standards, sight-distance requirements, and any corridor access-management rules; truck and loading-dock access needs wider drives and turning templates that the engineering standards may not grant by right. Utility coordination runs in parallel — franchise utilities have their own easement and clearance requirements that must be woven into the civil plans. The construction plans, once approved, become the permit documents the contractor builds and the inspector enforces, so their completeness at approval is what keeps the project out of field-change purgatory.",
