    slug: "moore-tornado-resistant-construction-standards",
    title: "What Are Moore's Tornado-Resistant Construction Standards?",
    description: "After the 2013 tornado, Moore adopted the nation's toughest residential wind code: 135 mph design, hurricane clips, continuous bracing, wind-rated garage doors.",
    h1: "What Are Moore's Tornado-Resistant Construction Standards?",
    answer: "Direct answer: after the May 2013 EF5 tornado, the City of Moore adopted enhanced residential building standards in March 2014 — the first and toughest municipal wind code of its kind in the country — requiring new homes to be built for 135 mph winds (EF2-level) instead of the conventional 90 mph, through roof sheathing upgrades, hurricane clips or framing anchors tying the roof to the walls, continuous plywood bracing on walls, and wind-resistant garage doors. The package of code changes added roughly $2 to $3 per square foot to new-home costs, and peer-reviewed benefit-cost analysis found the standards pay for themselves about three to one statewide. Moore is the AHJ, and its inspectors enforce the standards on every new home inside city limits.\n\nThe engineering logic is the continuous load path. Tornado damage investigations consistently show the same failure sequence: the garage door fails first, internal pressurization follows, the roof lifts off, and the walls rack and collapse. Moore's standards attack each link — a wind-rated garage door that stays in its tracks, metal connectors tying roof framing to wall top plates and walls to the foundation, wall bracing that keeps the box square under lateral load, and roof sheathing fastened to resist the uplift. No single item is exotic; the standard's power is requiring all of them together, on every house, so the whole subdivision performs instead of the occasional well-built outlier.\n\nThe 2015 test came quickly. An EF2 tornado struck Moore in March 2015, hitting homes built under the new standards at essentially their design wind speed. Post-storm engineering observations found those homes performed as designed — minor, quickly repairable damage where the robust structural system held — while nearby older construction fared worse. That real-world validation, plus the benefit-cost finding, is why Moore's code is cited nationally as the model for tornado-prone residential construction.\n\nMoore's leadership on shelters extends beyond the residential code. The city has required storm shelters in new schools and has pushed shelter requirements into commercial construction, so non-residential projects in Moore should verify the current local shelter amendments with the building department before design — the requirement can add a hardened safe room to the program and the budget. For builders working across the metro, the lesson is jurisdictional: Moore's standards apply inside Moore only, while Norman, Oklahoma City, and unincorporated Cleveland County each enforce their own adopted codes. The engineer designs to the AHJ that holds the parcel, and in Moore that means the enhanced wind package is not optional.",
